And we're currently running projects in Uganda, Kenya, Tanzania, Zambia, Malawi, Mozambique, and Rwanda — all African countries. And our main focus is on HIV care, TB care, mental health care, and harm reduction programs.

Now, this is very different from being a doctor in the UK because you have no access to any kind of infrastructure. There's no electricity, there's no clean water, there's no internet. So everything needs to be done differently. We need to do things like use solar power, bring in generators, set up mobile clinics, use satellite phones, etc. And so we're constantly having to innovate.

So, we're trying to deliver high-quality healthcare services in these challenging environments. But we're also trying to build capacity among local staff. We’re training doctors, nurses, pharmacists, and other healthcare workers. And we’re trying to create sustainable systems that can continue even after we leave.

We're also doing some research and evaluation work. We’ve published papers in peer-reviewed journals. We’re trying to understand how best to deliver care in low-resource settings. And we’re learning from the experiences of people living in prisons.

But despite all this effort, we still face major challenges. The biggest challenge is funding. We rely heavily on donors, which means our work is often short-term and unpredictable. This makes long-term planning difficult. Also, political instability in some countries affects our ability to operate. And sometimes, governments don’t prioritize prison healthcare, especially compared to other public health issues.

Still, we believe in the importance of providing quality healthcare to prisoners. It's not just a moral obligation, but also a practical necessity. Good health in prisons reduces violence, improves rehabilitation outcomes, and helps prevent disease transmission when prisoners return to communities.

That's why I’m here today: to share our story and hopefully inspire others to consider this important work. And if anyone wants to get involved, please reach out.

